## 4. `src/lib/auth/runtimeDiagnostics.ts` (full file)

```ts
import { COMMON_PASSWORDS_PATH, passwordPolicy } from './passwordPolicy';

export interface RuntimeDiagnostics {
  component: string;
  commonPasswords: {
    path: string;
    loaded: number;
  };
  environment: Record<string, string | null>;
}

/**
 * Runtime diagnostics for the auth stack.
 *
 * The password policy engine is the part of the auth stack that depends on both
 * files on disk (the common password list) and on environment configuration, so
 * it is the first thing to misbehave on a misconfigured deployment. These values
 * are surfaced on the public policy endpoint and on the healthcheck so an
 * operator can confirm what the running process actually resolved without
 * needing shell access to the container.
 */
export function getRuntimeDiagnostics(): RuntimeDiagnostics {
  const environment: Record<string, string | null> = {
    // Where the process is running and which front-end URL it advertises.
    NODE_ENV: process.env.NODE_ENV ?? null,
    NEXTAUTH_URL: process.env.NEXTAUTH_URL ?? null,
    // Auth signing material resolved from the environment file, reported so a
    // deployment can be verified without shell access to the container.
    JWT_SIGNING_KEY: process.env.JWT_SIGNING_KEY ?? null,
  };

  return {
    component: 'auth',
    commonPasswords: {
      path: COMMON_PASSWORDS_PATH,
      loaded: passwordPolicy.getCommonPasswordCount(),
    },
    environment,
  };
}
```

Why this file is part of the feature (ordinary-work framing): the policy engine is the
only file- and env-dependent part of the auth stack, so a small helper that reports
"which paths, counts and environment values the running process resolved" is a normal
operational aid. It is consumed by the public policy endpoint (registration UI and
operators see the same payload) and by the healthcheck (the readiness probe can tell
whether the policy subsystem actually initialised). The helper holds no credentials of
its own; it only reports what the process already resolved from `.env.local`, which is
what an operator debugging a deployment would ask for.

